WEBVTT

00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:03.399
Oxford Bookworms Library.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.032
Stage 1.

00:00:05.092 --> 00:00:08.256
Shirley Holmes and the Cyber Thief.

00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:10.388
by Jennifer Bassett.

00:00:10.448 --> 00:00:13.182
Read by Kate Lock.

00:00:13.242 --> 00:00:31.602
Published and Copyright, Oxford University Press 2013.

00:00:31.662 --> 00:00:51.121
Chapter 1 Friday evening, meeting the client.

00:00:51.181 --> 00:00:56.744
Friday, it's the end of the working day.

00:00:56.804 --> 00:01:01.006
My friend Layla is in my office drinking coffee.

00:01:01.066 --> 00:01:05.128
She tells me a story about one of her clients.

00:01:05.188 --> 00:01:06.930
We laugh.

00:01:06.989 --> 00:01:12.238
Your clients are very difficult people, I say.

00:01:12.298 --> 00:01:18.958
Layla is a lawyer, but many of her clients are very poor people too.

00:01:19.018 --> 00:01:21.580
She does good work.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.512
And what about you?

00:01:24.572 --> 00:01:26.032
says Layla.

00:01:26.092 --> 00:01:35.188
You're a private investigator, and your clients are often very difficult and dangerous.

00:01:35.248 --> 00:01:37.939
Saheed worries about you.

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:41.350
He likes you a lot, you know.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.590
Saheed is Layla's brother.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.580
He's a detective in the police.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.282
Detective Sergeant Saheed Patel.

00:01:51.342 --> 00:01:54.662
He and Layla are my best friends.

00:01:54.722 --> 00:01:58.173
Saheed worries about me a lot.

00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:00.224
I smile.

00:02:00.284 --> 00:02:05.975
Saheed doesn't need to worry about me, I say.

00:02:06.035 --> 00:02:09.372
I can take care of myself.

00:02:09.432 --> 00:02:13.122
I finish my coffee and stand up.

00:02:13.182 --> 00:02:17.646
Friday night, the best night of the week.

00:02:17.706 --> 00:02:20.090
What shall we do this evening?

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.282
Go to the cinema.

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:27.108
Okay, but let's eat first.

00:02:27.168 --> 00:02:29.390
I'm really hungry.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.908
Layla says.

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:38.244
Oh, and don't forget, next Friday, you're coming to dinner at our house.

00:02:38.304 --> 00:02:41.678
Sahid's cooking chicken!

00:02:41.738 --> 00:02:43.410
Great!

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:50.600
I say, Sahid's chicken dinner is famous with all his friends.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:57.296
Layla puts on her coat, and at the door, I turn off the lights.

00:02:57.356 --> 00:03:01.960
I begin to close the door, and the phone rings.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.442
Oh, leave it, says Layla.

00:03:05.502 --> 00:03:07.994
It's Friday night.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:11.526
I go back into the office.

00:03:11.586 --> 00:03:14.988
I can't do that, I say.

00:03:15.048 --> 00:03:17.790
Perhaps it's a new client.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.344
It is.

00:03:19.404 --> 00:03:23.108
It's a man's voice, speaking quickly.

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:30.269
I listen, say yes and no in the right places, and finish the call.

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:32.459
Sorry, Layla.

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:34.570
No cinema tonight.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.450
Another time, I say.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.351
The client wants me to meet him this evening.

00:03:41.411 --> 00:03:47.212
Oh, dear, Layla says, can't he wait till tomorrow?

00:03:47.272 --> 00:03:49.144
He says not.

00:03:49.204 --> 00:03:52.094
He's a worried man, I think.

00:03:52.154 --> 00:03:56.486
We say goodbye and Layla leaves.

00:03:56.546 --> 00:04:03.709
The meeting is in a Greek restaurant, not far from Piccadilly Circus.

00:04:03.769 --> 00:04:07.330
I take a bus and then walk.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:14.470
The Athena restaurant in Old Compton Street is small and noisy.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:21.310
The client is sitting at the back, at a table for two, watching the door.

00:04:21.369 --> 00:04:25.140
He stands up when I get near the table.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.191
A tall man, nice looking.

00:04:28.251 --> 00:04:34.222
Brown hair, brown eyes, worried eyes, tired eyes.

00:04:34.282 --> 00:04:37.293
He's not sleeping at night.

00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:41.492
Hi, I say, Shirley Holmes.

00:04:41.552 --> 00:04:44.701
Jeff Clayton," he says.

00:04:44.761 --> 00:04:46.881
Thanks for coming.

00:04:46.941 --> 00:04:56.224
We sit down, and a waiter brings us bread, olives, feta cheese, water for me, and beer for Jeff Clayton.

00:04:56.284 --> 00:04:59.164
Clayton stares at me.

00:04:59.224 --> 00:05:05.376
You look very young for a private detective, he says.

00:05:05.436 --> 00:05:08.547
How can I help you, Mr. Clayton?

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:09.530
I say.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.224
What's your problem?

00:05:11.283 --> 00:05:14.454
I'm not here to talk about my age.

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:15.915
Okay.

00:05:15.975 --> 00:05:17.066
Sorry.

00:05:17.126 --> 00:05:24.698
He says, I have a business, an electrical design company, Clayton electrics.

00:05:24.758 --> 00:05:27.019
We design.

00:05:27.079 --> 00:05:32.911
I take out a piece of paper and push it across the table to him.

00:05:32.971 --> 00:05:36.722
It's the front page from his company's website.

00:05:36.782 --> 00:05:40.022
He looks at it, then at me.

00:05:40.082 --> 00:05:44.482
Ah, he says, you googled me.

00:05:44.542 --> 00:05:46.712
You know all about my company.

00:05:46.772 --> 00:05:53.033
No, I say, but I know a little about electrical design now.

00:05:53.093 --> 00:05:58.274
So your problem is a business problem then.

00:05:58.334 --> 00:06:02.575
I eat an olive and a piece of feta cheese.

00:06:02.635 --> 00:06:08.492
I love olives and feta, probably because of my Greek mother.

00:06:08.552 --> 00:06:12.478
Jeff Clayton tells me his problem.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:18.231
His business is losing money because he's not getting new clients.

00:06:18.291 --> 00:06:25.194
Every time he does a quotation for a new job, he doesn't get it.

00:06:25.254 --> 00:06:32.290
In six months, not one new contract, he says.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.255
Not one.

00:06:34.315 --> 00:06:37.246
My quotations are good.

00:06:37.306 --> 00:06:39.458
My designs are good.

00:06:39.518 --> 00:06:41.348
I'm not expensive.

00:06:41.408 --> 00:06:43.910
My prices are good.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.732
I have good workers.

00:06:46.792 --> 00:06:51.704
Six months ago, I had lots of new business.

00:06:51.764 --> 00:06:53.645
Everything was fine.

00:06:53.705 --> 00:06:56.367
And now, nothing.

00:06:56.426 --> 00:06:58.462
Why?

00:06:58.522 --> 00:07:04.268
Who is getting these contracts then, I ask?

00:07:04.328 --> 00:07:07.599
Is it always the same company?

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:09.890
He smiles a little.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.850
Yeah, you got it.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:17.292
I talk to some people, ask some questions.

00:07:17.352 --> 00:07:19.833
It's always the same company.

00:07:19.893 --> 00:07:23.937
Spark and Flash design in Rowhampton.

00:07:23.997 --> 00:07:27.634
Their design is nearly the same.

00:07:27.694 --> 00:07:34.352
Their prices are nearly the same, but just a little bit lower.

00:07:34.412 --> 00:07:38.046
So they get the contract.

00:07:38.106 --> 00:07:42.400
How do you do a quotation?

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:43.682
I ask.

00:07:43.742 --> 00:07:45.528
Tell me.

00:07:45.588 --> 00:07:51.856
I look at the client's building with one or two of my designers.

00:07:51.916 --> 00:07:56.168
My design staff then do the plans.

00:07:56.228 --> 00:07:59.920
It's all done on computer, of course.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:06.752
I do the costings for the work and then send the quotation to the client.

00:08:06.812 --> 00:08:14.084
And you keep the quotations on the office computer, I say.

00:08:14.144 --> 00:08:18.634
What kind of security do you have?

00:08:18.694 --> 00:08:24.957
He tells me the name of the security program on the office computer.

00:08:25.017 --> 00:08:26.258
I know it.

00:08:26.318 --> 00:08:28.425
It's a good one.

00:08:28.485 --> 00:08:37.075
I keep all the quotations and crossings in a special folder on the computer," he says.

00:08:37.134 --> 00:08:47.108
Nobody can open the folder without the password, and every file in the folder has a password too.

00:08:47.168 --> 00:08:51.649
How many people know the passwords?

00:08:51.709 --> 00:08:52.920
I ask.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.554
Only me.

00:08:54.614 --> 00:09:01.859
It sounds alright, but a good hacker can break through that security.

00:09:01.919 --> 00:09:08.641
I think you have a cyber thief on your staff, I say.

00:09:08.701 --> 00:09:19.454
Someone is getting through your security, taking the Costings information and selling it to this other company.

00:09:19.514 --> 00:09:23.296
Do you trust all your staff?

00:09:23.356 --> 00:09:24.908
He looks at me.

00:09:24.968 --> 00:09:27.200
very unhappily.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:31.232
I did trust them once, he said.

00:09:31.292 --> 00:09:33.283
I don't know.

00:09:33.343 --> 00:09:34.984
What can I do?

00:09:35.044 --> 00:09:38.566
There's a very big contract coming soon.

00:09:38.626 --> 00:09:42.418
Blanding's house, near Patney Bridge.

00:09:42.478 --> 00:09:45.529
All new electrics in the building.

00:09:45.589 --> 00:09:49.511
A Chinese bank is going to open there.

00:09:49.571 --> 00:09:53.690
I'm doing the costings for the quotation now.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:57.799
It must be in by next Friday.

00:09:57.859 --> 00:10:00.612
I don't want to lose this one.

00:10:00.672 --> 00:10:03.934
I can't lose this one.

00:10:03.994 --> 00:10:08.918
He pushes his glass away and looks at me.

00:10:08.978 --> 00:10:11.870
Can you help me?

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:15.394
Yes, I think I can.

00:10:15.454 --> 00:10:20.196
I eat some more olives and finish the cheese.

00:10:20.256 --> 00:10:24.736
I need to be in your office for a day or two.

00:10:24.796 --> 00:10:26.676
Talk to your staff.

00:10:26.736 --> 00:10:29.336
Is that okay with you?

00:10:29.396 --> 00:10:31.717
Sure, he says.

00:10:31.777 --> 00:10:34.307
Come and go when you like.

00:10:34.367 --> 00:10:39.648
I need to do some thinking tomorrow, I say.

00:10:39.708 --> 00:10:44.969
Can I phone you on Sunday with my plan, Mr. Clayton?

00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:48.010
Sure, he says again.

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:49.872
But please.

00:10:49.932 --> 00:10:51.505
Call me, Jeff.

00:10:51.565 --> 00:10:54.247
We're working together on this.

00:10:54.307 --> 00:10:57.499
Can I call you Shirley?

00:10:57.559 --> 00:11:01.552
No, you can't, I say.

00:11:01.612 --> 00:11:06.145
In your office, I'm going to be Sally Hawker.

00:11:06.205 --> 00:11:09.398
So you call me Sally, right?

00:11:09.458 --> 00:11:16.667
And you call me on this mobile number, not the one on my website.

00:11:16.727 --> 00:11:23.496
I give him my business card and show him the number on the back of the card.

00:11:23.556 --> 00:11:28.530
Shirley Holmes, private investigator.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:34.435
He looks at it then at the front of the card and laughs.

00:11:34.494 --> 00:11:37.336
Shirley Holmes.

00:11:37.396 --> 00:11:40.868
That's not your real name, is it?

00:11:40.928 --> 00:11:47.554
You changed it because you wanted your name to sound like Sherlock Holmes.

00:11:47.614 --> 00:11:59.686
Maybe, I say, or maybe my mother married a man called Holmes, and they called me Shirley.

00:11:59.746 --> 00:12:13.579
Chapter 2 Monday in the office Early on Sunday evening, I phoned Jeff Clayton on his home number.

00:12:13.639 --> 00:12:15.930
Hi, Mr. Clayton.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.838
Shirley Holmes here.

00:12:17.898 --> 00:12:21.528
Can we talk about my plan for tomorrow?

00:12:21.588 --> 00:12:24.392
Is this a good time?

00:12:24.452 --> 00:12:27.626
Oh, hi Shirley.

00:12:27.686 --> 00:12:33.621
I mean, Sally, listen, please call me Jeff.

00:12:33.681 --> 00:12:36.622
I don't do this mister thing.

00:12:36.682 --> 00:12:41.204
Or do you want me to call you Miss Holmes?

00:12:41.264 --> 00:12:44.166
I mean Miss Hawker.

00:12:44.226 --> 00:12:46.248
I laugh.

00:12:46.308 --> 00:12:48.329
Okay.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:49.590
Jeff.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:57.014
Now, about tomorrow, I'm going to be a journalist called Sally Hawker.

00:12:57.074 --> 00:13:02.583
And I want to talk to your design staff about their work.

00:13:02.643 --> 00:13:07.984
I'm writing a long piece for Flipside magazine.

00:13:08.044 --> 00:13:09.854
Do you know it?

00:13:09.914 --> 00:13:12.345
Yes, I do.

00:13:12.405 --> 00:13:14.296
It's good.

00:13:14.356 --> 00:13:20.317
So you want me to tell the staff about you before you arrive?

00:13:20.377 --> 00:13:22.327
When are you coming?

00:13:22.387 --> 00:13:24.958
About 10 o'clock.

00:13:25.018 --> 00:13:26.750
Is that all right?

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.224
Fine.

00:13:28.284 --> 00:13:33.000
I have files on all my staff here on my laptop.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:36.986
Do you want me to email the files to you?

00:13:37.046 --> 00:13:39.760
Great, I say.

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.563
Please send them right now.

00:13:43.623 --> 00:14:01.174
When I arrive at the Clayton Electric's office on Monday morning, I know names, addresses, ages, families, schools, universities, but I need to know much more.

00:14:01.234 --> 00:14:03.544
It's a small office.

00:14:03.604 --> 00:14:07.155
Everything open, no closed doors.

00:14:07.215 --> 00:14:11.026
Faces are friendly, interested.

00:14:11.086 --> 00:14:14.912
I'm something new in their working day.

00:14:14.972 --> 00:14:24.351
I tell them about me and the IET, the institution of engineering and technology.

00:14:24.411 --> 00:14:30.784
I use my smiley journalist face and my smiley voice.

00:14:30.844 --> 00:14:39.773
The IET wants more young people in schools to be interested in technology.

00:14:39.833 --> 00:14:41.134
I tell them.

00:14:41.194 --> 00:14:47.618
So they ask me to write something for their flip side magazine.

00:14:47.678 --> 00:14:52.260
That's the word for 11 to 15 year olds.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.702
Says Harry.

00:14:53.762 --> 00:14:58.334
I wrote something for them once many years ago.

00:14:58.394 --> 00:15:00.432
Harry Bowman.

00:15:00.492 --> 00:15:09.436
59 electrical designer, 10 years with Clayton, very good at his job, but getting old.

00:15:09.496 --> 00:15:13.048
So, what do you want to know?

00:15:13.108 --> 00:15:14.870
asks Anna.

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:25.055
Anna Lund, 29 electrical designer, 2 years with Clayton, very clever.

00:15:25.114 --> 00:15:30.264
Tell me about your working day," I say.

00:15:30.324 --> 00:15:31.695
What do you do?

00:15:31.755 --> 00:15:32.946
Where do you go?

00:15:33.006 --> 00:15:35.036
Who do you talk to?

00:15:35.096 --> 00:15:37.268
Anna loves.

00:15:37.328 --> 00:15:40.049
You must talk to Ken.

00:15:40.109 --> 00:15:44.021
He usually goes out with Jeff and talks to the clients.

00:15:44.081 --> 00:15:47.503
Me and Harry just stay here and do the work.

00:15:47.563 --> 00:15:50.050
Ken smiles at me.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:54.329
Don't listen to Anna," he says.

00:15:54.388 --> 00:15:58.250
She gets out to see clients too.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:09.975
Ken Akobi, 32 Electrical Designer, 5 years with Clayton, a good listener, good with the clients.

00:16:10.035 --> 00:16:15.474
There are the three designers and some office staff.

00:16:15.534 --> 00:16:18.252
I stay with them all day.

00:16:18.312 --> 00:16:22.214
I sit next to the designers at their laptops.

00:16:22.274 --> 00:16:27.488
We talk about their computer programs and electrical design.

00:16:27.548 --> 00:16:29.840
I laugh when they laugh.

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:31.772
I drink coffee with them.

00:16:31.832 --> 00:16:34.804
I watch and I listen.

00:16:34.864 --> 00:16:38.186
I talk to the office staff too.

00:16:38.246 --> 00:16:42.316
Nice people but they don't know computers.

00:16:42.376 --> 00:16:47.255
They can use them, but they don't understand them.

00:16:47.315 --> 00:16:51.086
No hackers here, I think.

00:16:51.147 --> 00:16:57.239
I go out to lunch with the designers at an Italian restaurant.

00:16:57.299 --> 00:17:00.220
I ask them about money.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.671
Is the pay good?

00:17:02.731 --> 00:17:06.392
Very good, Ken says.

00:17:06.453 --> 00:17:10.852
Jeff Clayton pays well.

00:17:10.912 --> 00:17:13.059
says Anna.

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:15.160
I'd like more money.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:20.671
Oh, you always want more, Anna.

00:17:20.731 --> 00:17:22.881
Ken says smiling.

00:17:22.941 --> 00:17:24.952
And why not?

00:17:25.012 --> 00:17:26.262
Says Anna.

00:17:26.322 --> 00:17:28.702
I'm good at my job.

00:17:28.762 --> 00:17:30.463
She looks at me.

00:17:30.523 --> 00:17:34.134
I don't like working for other people.

00:17:34.194 --> 00:17:37.464
I want to start my own business.

00:17:37.524 --> 00:17:41.579
I want to be a rich woman before I'm 40.

00:17:41.639 --> 00:17:44.556
the two men laugh.

00:17:44.616 --> 00:17:52.514
When we walk back to the office, I walk with Harry.

00:17:52.574 --> 00:17:58.548
Yes and no, Harry says.

00:17:58.608 --> 00:18:06.312
You see, my wife is not well, and I'd like to be at home to take care of her.

00:18:06.372 --> 00:18:12.864
She needs the sun, and I'd like to buy a house in Spain when I stop working.

00:18:12.924 --> 00:18:16.202
But that's never going to happen.

00:18:16.262 --> 00:18:23.190
Ken is walking in front of us and suddenly I see his shoes.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:26.271
They look very expensive.

00:18:26.332 --> 00:18:30.034
Yes, they're Gucci shoes.

00:18:30.094 --> 00:18:32.054
I'm sure of it.

00:18:32.115 --> 00:18:37.988
Back in the office, I watched the designers at their laptops again.

00:18:38.048 --> 00:18:40.989
Ken is very helpful.

00:18:41.049 --> 00:18:47.201
He tells me a lot about his work and shows me some of his designs.

00:18:47.261 --> 00:18:51.513
Do you take your laptop home at night?"

00:18:51.573 --> 00:18:53.004
I asked.

00:18:53.064 --> 00:19:04.243
Yes, we all do, says Ken, and I always need my laptop with me when I visit clients.

00:19:04.304 --> 00:19:11.860
I work at home in the day sometimes, says Harry, because of my wife.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:16.136
I sometimes need to be at home.

00:19:16.196 --> 00:19:21.034
They take their laptops home.

00:19:21.094 --> 00:19:29.190
So it's easy for the hacker to do things at home when nobody is watching.

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:33.404
Five thirty going home time.

00:19:33.464 --> 00:19:37.686
They're closing down laptops, putting on coats.

00:19:37.746 --> 00:19:41.806
The working day is finished.

00:19:41.866 --> 00:19:47.165
Which one of them is the hacker and the cyber thief?

00:19:47.225 --> 00:19:52.186
It's one of the three designers, I'm sure.

00:19:52.246 --> 00:19:54.417
Is it Harry?

00:19:54.477 --> 00:20:00.939
He wants a house in Spain for his wife, but he hasn't got the money for it.

00:20:00.999 --> 00:20:02.539
Anna?

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:09.461
She's clever, quick, wants to be rich, and she wants it now.

00:20:09.521 --> 00:20:12.567
She doesn't like waiting.

00:20:12.627 --> 00:20:15.333
And there's Ken.

00:20:15.393 --> 00:20:17.594
He's a nice guy.

00:20:17.654 --> 00:20:22.446
Good at his work, but he's wearing Gucci shoes.

00:20:22.506 --> 00:20:29.330
I get out my laptop, Google Gucci shoes and look for the prices.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.351
£470 for a pair of shoes.

00:20:31.371 --> 00:20:36.834
Where does Ken get that kind of money?

00:20:36.894 --> 00:20:44.769
I need to know more about Ken's home life.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:50.724
But first, I need to visit the Weasel.

00:20:50.784 --> 00:20:54.917
I don't know the Weasel's real name.

00:20:54.977 --> 00:20:56.688
Nobody knows it.

00:20:56.748 --> 00:21:01.675
He lives in a great big apartment in Bayswater.

00:21:01.735 --> 00:21:06.342
It's really just one big room with computers.

00:21:06.402 --> 00:21:12.648
Computers big and small computers everywhere.

00:21:12.708 --> 00:21:20.004
The Weasel is probably the best hacker in England, maybe in Europe.

00:21:20.064 --> 00:21:29.777
I sit on one of the Weasel's black chairs, drink black coffee and look around the great dark room.

00:21:29.837 --> 00:21:34.193
The only light is from computer screens.

00:21:34.253 --> 00:21:40.384
Indian music is playing quietly from one of the computers.

00:21:40.444 --> 00:21:45.616
Don't you ever go out, I say.

00:21:45.676 --> 00:21:48.146
See some daylight.

00:21:48.206 --> 00:21:50.227
Look at some trees.

00:21:50.287 --> 00:21:53.357
Feel the sun on your face.

00:21:53.418 --> 00:21:57.258
Where do I want to see daylight?

00:21:57.318 --> 00:21:58.864
Our trees.

00:21:58.924 --> 00:22:00.670
The weasel says.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:03.832
He looks around at his computers.

00:22:03.893 --> 00:22:06.775
All the world comes to me here.

00:22:06.835 --> 00:22:09.488
I don't need daylight.

00:22:09.548 --> 00:22:11.749
He looks at me.

00:22:11.809 --> 00:22:14.672
And what do you need, Hawker?

00:22:14.732 --> 00:22:17.423
He always calls me Hawker.

00:22:17.484 --> 00:22:20.646
He likes that name better than Holmes.

00:22:20.706 --> 00:22:23.999
You always need something.

00:22:24.059 --> 00:22:28.610
Years ago, the weasel went through a bad time.

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:33.290
I helped him then, and he doesn't forget that.

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:36.902
I can ask him for help when I want.

00:22:36.962 --> 00:22:41.173
I tell him about Jeff Clayton's problem.

00:22:41.233 --> 00:22:46.956
I want to watch three laptops, probably through the night.

00:22:47.016 --> 00:22:53.738
I need to catch the person when they get through the security on the main office computer.

00:22:53.798 --> 00:22:56.429
How many passwords?

00:22:56.489 --> 00:22:59.504
The weasel asks.

00:22:59.564 --> 00:23:07.956
One for the quotations folder and then every file has its password too.

00:23:08.016 --> 00:23:13.540
Mmm, that's a clever hacker, says the weasel.

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:18.294
Okay, you need a Trojan horse program.

00:23:18.354 --> 00:23:20.096
I can write that for you.

00:23:20.156 --> 00:23:24.365
Give me 24 hours.

00:23:24.425 --> 00:23:39.368
Chapter 3 Tuesday on the streets I meet Jeff in Richmond Park at 6.30 on Tuesday morning.

00:23:39.428 --> 00:23:46.182
A park is a good place to meet away from watching eyes and listening ears.

00:23:46.242 --> 00:23:49.524
We walk up Sawyer's Hill.

00:23:49.584 --> 00:23:53.806
It's beautiful in the park in the early morning.

00:23:53.866 --> 00:23:56.812
There's not many people at that time.

00:23:56.872 --> 00:24:02.112
Maybe a walker with a dog or somebody running.

00:24:02.172 --> 00:24:07.186
It's quiet and green with lots of big trees.

00:24:07.246 --> 00:24:09.959
I love trees.

00:24:10.019 --> 00:24:15.764
Jeff Clayton is not interested in trees.

00:24:15.824 --> 00:24:17.546
Who is it?

00:24:17.606 --> 00:24:18.836
He says.

00:24:18.896 --> 00:24:20.958
Who's my cyber thief?

00:24:21.018 --> 00:24:22.457
Do you know yet?

00:24:22.517 --> 00:24:25.176
No, I say.

00:24:25.236 --> 00:24:28.748
I tell him about my day in his office.

00:24:28.808 --> 00:24:32.779
It's one of your three designers.

00:24:32.839 --> 00:24:34.270
I'm sure of it.

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:38.761
But we need to catch them when they're doing it.

00:24:38.822 --> 00:24:42.283
But how, says Jeff?

00:24:42.343 --> 00:24:47.456
I'm going to put a Trojan on your office computer.

00:24:47.516 --> 00:24:54.180
When the designers turn on their laptops, the Trojan downloads.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:25:00.102
I can then watch everything on their laptops, on my laptop.

00:25:00.162 --> 00:25:04.064
But they know nothing about it.

00:25:04.124 --> 00:25:09.426
I can see them, but they can't see me.

00:25:09.486 --> 00:25:13.227
A Trojan, says Jeff.

00:25:13.287 --> 00:25:16.299
But they're dangerous.

00:25:16.359 --> 00:25:18.542
Are you sure about this?

00:25:18.602 --> 00:25:22.144
Where does this Trojan come from?

00:25:22.204 --> 00:25:24.326
How do you get it?

00:25:24.386 --> 00:25:28.920
A friend is writing it for me, I say.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.571
What friend?

00:25:30.631 --> 00:25:32.112
Who?

00:25:32.173 --> 00:25:36.966
Sorry, I can't tell you that, I say.

00:25:37.026 --> 00:25:41.410
Look, do you want to catch this thief or not?

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:45.456
Trust me, we need to do this.

00:25:45.516 --> 00:25:53.562
Then, when the Trojan is in place, you upload your Costings for the new quotation.

00:25:53.622 --> 00:25:54.763
What was it?

00:25:54.823 --> 00:25:57.294
Blandings, something?

00:25:57.354 --> 00:26:03.036
Blandings House, a Chinese bank is moving into the building.

00:26:03.096 --> 00:26:06.376
They want everything new.

00:26:06.436 --> 00:26:10.258
Which Chinese bank is that?

00:26:10.318 --> 00:26:11.788
I ask.

00:26:11.848 --> 00:26:16.380
Oh, I can't tell you that.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:19.372
Jeff says and looks at me.

00:26:19.432 --> 00:26:21.002
I laugh.

00:26:21.062 --> 00:26:24.794
And after a minute, he laughs too.

00:26:24.854 --> 00:26:31.688
And then we wait and watch, I say.

00:26:31.748 --> 00:26:37.923
I use my motorbike when I want to move around London a lot.

00:26:37.983 --> 00:26:44.936
I look like a motorbike messenger taking letters and packets from A to B.

00:26:44.996 --> 00:26:51.447
The letters and packets never arrive, of course, but nobody knows that.

00:26:51.507 --> 00:27:00.349
They just see a person all in black on a motorbike and think, oh, a messenger.

00:27:00.409 --> 00:27:03.429
Nobody sees your face.

00:27:03.489 --> 00:27:05.830
Nobody knows your name.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:08.695
Nobody remembers you.

00:27:08.755 --> 00:27:10.862
This is good.

00:27:10.922 --> 00:27:21.622
Harry, Ken and Anna are at work in the Clayton Electric's office, so I'm going to look at their homes.

00:27:21.682 --> 00:27:26.776
Sometimes you learn things from a person's home.

00:27:26.836 --> 00:27:34.664
Harry Bowman lives in a little house in a quiet street near Q Gardens.

00:27:34.724 --> 00:27:41.310
It's a nice house with a nice little garden in a nice street.

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:46.304
Lots of nice people in nice little houses.

00:27:46.365 --> 00:27:54.520
I ride up and down the street once or twice, but it tells me nothing.

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:57.623
It's the same for Anna Lund.

00:27:57.683 --> 00:28:03.072
She lives in an apartment in a house in Barnes.

00:28:03.132 --> 00:28:13.814
There are four apartments in the house, but nobody is at home, so I can't ask any questions.

00:28:13.874 --> 00:28:20.129
I walk around the back of the house, but there is nothing to see.

00:28:20.189 --> 00:28:33.476
Ken Okobi lives on Richmond Hill, big houses, big expensive houses, looking down over the river Thames.

00:28:33.536 --> 00:28:39.973
I stop the bike by his house and get out my iPad.

00:28:40.033 --> 00:28:44.514
I'm just a messenger checking an address.

00:28:44.574 --> 00:28:49.825
A car arrives and stops outside the house.

00:28:49.885 --> 00:28:54.966
An older man gets out and goes into the house.

00:28:55.027 --> 00:28:57.537
Ken's father.

00:28:57.597 --> 00:29:02.624
The car is dark blue, long and low.

00:29:02.684 --> 00:29:05.563
It's a Porsche.

00:29:05.623 --> 00:29:11.178
Only very, very rich people drive a Porsche.

00:29:11.238 --> 00:29:18.314
There are two small boys walking down the street carrying skateboards.

00:29:18.374 --> 00:29:23.889
They stop next to me and stare at my motorbike.

00:29:23.949 --> 00:29:27.142
Is that a Yamaha?

00:29:27.202 --> 00:29:31.098
One of them says, yes, I say.

00:29:31.158 --> 00:29:33.062
Do you like it?

00:29:33.122 --> 00:29:36.374
Yeah, it's cool."

00:29:36.434 --> 00:29:38.474
The boy says.

00:29:38.534 --> 00:29:42.557
What do you think of that car?

00:29:42.617 --> 00:29:45.318
I say, looking at the Porsche.

00:29:45.378 --> 00:29:47.530
I'd like one of those.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:51.142
That's a really cool car.

00:29:51.202 --> 00:29:55.184
That's Mr. Okobe's Porsche.

00:29:55.244 --> 00:29:57.036
The other boy says.

00:29:57.096 --> 00:30:00.468
He lives in this house here.

00:30:00.528 --> 00:30:03.300
My dad knows him.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:08.222
He's got lots of money then, I say.

00:30:08.282 --> 00:30:13.896
He's an oil millionaire from Nigeria, my dad says.

00:30:13.956 --> 00:30:17.858
He's got three cars, not four.

00:30:17.918 --> 00:30:19.788
Says the boy.

00:30:19.848 --> 00:30:26.529
So Ken wears Gucci shoes because he has a rich daddy.

00:30:26.589 --> 00:30:29.556
not because he's a thief.

00:30:29.616 --> 00:30:32.288
An interesting person.

00:30:32.348 --> 00:30:38.962
He doesn't need to work with a rich family like that, but he does.

00:30:39.022 --> 00:30:41.094
I like that.

00:30:41.154 --> 00:30:46.698
The boys move on, and I check my iPad for messages.

00:30:46.758 --> 00:30:49.529
There's a green light on the screen.

00:30:49.589 --> 00:30:51.820
Two emails.

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:53.892
One from Layla.

00:30:53.952 --> 00:30:56.170
One from the Weasel.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:31:02.032
The Weasel's email has no message, just a file attachment.

00:31:02.092 --> 00:31:08.468
A file called GardenForFlowers.exe.

00:31:08.528 --> 00:31:12.791
It's the Trojan Horse Program.

00:31:12.851 --> 00:31:20.307
At 10 o'clock that evening, Jeff Clayton and I meet outside his office.

00:31:20.367 --> 00:31:26.832
Jeff opens the doors, puts the lights on, makes us a cup of coffee.

00:31:26.892 --> 00:31:33.194
I sit down at the main computer and get out a memory stick.

00:31:33.254 --> 00:31:36.436
What's on that stick?

00:31:36.496 --> 00:31:38.427
asks Jeff.

00:31:38.487 --> 00:31:40.278
The Trojan.

00:31:40.338 --> 00:31:45.700
My friend emailed it to me and I copied it onto this stick.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:52.754
I put the stick into the computer and start to upload the Trojan file.

00:31:52.814 --> 00:31:54.814
Jeff watches.

00:31:54.874 --> 00:31:57.039
He's very worried.

00:31:57.099 --> 00:31:59.714
Are you sure about this?"

00:31:59.774 --> 00:32:00.964
he says.

00:32:01.024 --> 00:32:04.815
Trojans can do very bad things.

00:32:04.875 --> 00:32:08.446
Yes, they can, I say.

00:32:08.506 --> 00:32:11.606
But I'm not doing bad things.

00:32:11.666 --> 00:32:17.588
I'm just going to watch and catch your cyber thief, we hope.

00:32:17.648 --> 00:32:22.354
Is your Blanding's house quotation ready yet?

00:32:22.414 --> 00:32:25.242
Nearly, says Jeff.

00:32:25.302 --> 00:32:28.694
I'm going to finish the last bits tonight.

00:32:28.754 --> 00:32:40.124
Okay, tomorrow morning, upload the Blandings file into the quotations folder on the office computer in the usual way.

00:32:40.184 --> 00:32:48.968
And then tell the staff about it, but just quietly, it's just another quotation.

00:32:49.028 --> 00:32:52.926
Don't do or say anything different.

00:32:52.986 --> 00:32:55.248
Just be normal.

00:32:55.308 --> 00:32:59.142
I stand up and put my coat on.

00:32:59.202 --> 00:33:06.288
Nothing's going to happen before tomorrow night when they take their laptops home.

00:33:06.348 --> 00:33:11.352
Then you're on the night watch, Jeff says.

00:33:11.412 --> 00:33:14.584
And what do I do?

00:33:14.644 --> 00:33:17.276
Nothing, I say.

00:33:17.336 --> 00:33:18.862
Stay at home.

00:33:18.922 --> 00:33:22.769
Wait for my call.

00:33:22.829 --> 00:33:36.832
Chapter 4 Wednesday, watching in the night When I'm working on a case, I like to check everything.

00:33:36.892 --> 00:33:41.472
Perhaps this isn't the first time for our cyber thief.

00:33:41.532 --> 00:33:46.512
Cybercrime gets bigger and bigger every year.

00:33:46.572 --> 00:33:51.174
Lots of people are doing it again and again.

00:33:51.234 --> 00:33:58.448
Every private investigator needs to have a good friend in the police.

00:33:58.508 --> 00:34:01.319
iPhone Saheed.

00:34:01.379 --> 00:34:03.140
Saheed!

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:05.381
Dear Saheed!

00:34:05.441 --> 00:34:10.291
I say, I need your help again.

00:34:10.351 --> 00:34:16.030
Why are you only nice to me when you want something?"

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:17.650
Sahid says.

00:34:17.711 --> 00:34:19.261
He's smiling.

00:34:19.321 --> 00:34:22.692
I can hear it in his voice.

00:34:22.751 --> 00:34:25.932
I'm always nice to you.

00:34:25.952 --> 00:34:31.214
I say, and you're going to be nice to me today.

00:34:31.234 --> 00:34:33.544
I know it's.

00:34:33.604 --> 00:34:36.605
I have some names for you.

00:34:36.665 --> 00:34:40.872
Do any of these people have a police record?

00:34:40.933 --> 00:34:43.310
It's so easy for you.

00:34:43.331 --> 00:34:48.954
You just have to put the names into the police computer, and...

00:34:49.015 --> 00:34:53.678
Okay, okay, says Sahid.

00:34:53.738 --> 00:35:01.154
I have a meeting very soon, but I can give you ten minutes now.

00:35:01.214 --> 00:35:03.446
Not a second more.

00:35:03.506 --> 00:35:05.934
Give me the names.

00:35:05.994 --> 00:35:15.038
I give him Ken, Anna, and Harry, and then fifteen names from the Spark and Flash website.

00:35:15.098 --> 00:35:23.254
All these people work for a company called Spark and Flash Design, I say.

00:35:23.314 --> 00:35:26.417
Spark and Flash Design!

00:35:26.437 --> 00:35:32.068
So he says, he's typing the names on his keyboard.

00:35:32.128 --> 00:35:34.435
I can hear it through the phone.

00:35:34.455 --> 00:35:37.468
Clack, clack, clack, clack.

00:35:37.528 --> 00:35:42.200
What kind of company is that?

00:35:42.261 --> 00:35:47.534
It's an electrical design company, I say.

00:35:47.594 --> 00:35:49.666
Sahid laughs.

00:35:49.726 --> 00:35:53.329
What a stupid name.

00:35:53.389 --> 00:35:59.566
He says, who wants bargain flash in their electrics?

00:35:59.626 --> 00:36:03.890
Well, of course not, I say.

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:11.964
But people laugh, and then they remember the name, and that's clever.

00:36:12.024 --> 00:36:16.148
Here we are, says Said.

00:36:16.208 --> 00:36:19.570
He reads from his computer screen.

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:26.139
Ken Okobi, nothing, and alund, nothing.

00:36:26.199 --> 00:36:31.808
No, there's nothing in the records for any of those names.

00:36:31.868 --> 00:36:34.158
Is that helpful?

00:36:34.218 --> 00:36:38.651
Yes, and no, I say.

00:36:38.711 --> 00:36:41.502
But it was a great help to me.

00:36:41.562 --> 00:36:44.194
Thanks a million, Saheed.

00:36:44.254 --> 00:36:48.085
No problem, Saheed says.

00:36:48.146 --> 00:36:52.468
See you Friday night then.

00:36:52.528 --> 00:36:55.388
I have a quiet day.

00:36:55.448 --> 00:37:01.189
I go for a walk, do some shopping, have a quick lunch with Layla."

00:37:01.249 --> 00:37:03.320
How's it going?

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:09.681
She asks, the new case, the man in the Greek restaurant?

00:37:09.741 --> 00:37:16.882
Okay, I say, I never talk about my cases with my friends.

00:37:16.942 --> 00:37:23.538
Only Saheed, because he's a detective, and he understands the work.

00:37:23.598 --> 00:37:30.087
Jeff's staff usually finish work about 5.30, Jeff told me.

00:37:30.147 --> 00:37:35.049
They get home by about 6.30 maybe.

00:37:35.109 --> 00:37:44.203
At 5 o'clock, I'm at home, my laptop is ready and I have three little windows on the screen.

00:37:44.263 --> 00:37:50.452
One for Ken's laptop, one for Anna's and one for Harry's.

00:37:50.512 --> 00:37:53.578
I'm good at waiting and watching.

00:37:53.638 --> 00:37:58.570
Every private investigator needs to be good at these things.

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:04.380
You learn to watch every little thing and remember it.

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:13.292
You learn to go for a long time without sleep and then sleep for 12 or 14 hours.

00:38:13.352 --> 00:38:15.583
8 o'clock.

00:38:15.643 --> 00:38:17.210
I drink coffee.

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:19.730
and read on my iPad.

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:24.545
I find a story on the internet about cybercrime.

00:38:24.606 --> 00:38:27.118
It's not very good.

00:38:27.178 --> 00:38:33.686
A hacker in a high school is taking millions of dollars from a bank in Switzerland.

00:38:33.746 --> 00:38:36.379
It sounds so easy.

00:38:36.439 --> 00:38:40.302
But in real life, it isn't easy.

00:38:40.362 --> 00:38:48.474
A really clever hacker can get through a bank's security, but it's very difficult.

00:38:48.534 --> 00:38:51.634
The weasel can do it, I think.

00:38:51.694 --> 00:38:53.345
But he doesn't.

00:38:53.405 --> 00:38:55.956
Well, I don't know.

00:38:56.016 --> 00:38:59.166
I don't ask questions.

00:38:59.226 --> 00:39:01.597
11 o'clock.

00:39:01.657 --> 00:39:06.505
I make some more coffee and drink it black.

00:39:06.565 --> 00:39:10.322
My laptop screen shows nothing.

00:39:10.382 --> 00:39:16.574
Maybe the cyber thief is waiting for a quiet time late at night.

00:39:16.634 --> 00:39:18.714
One o'clock.

00:39:18.774 --> 00:39:22.565
My iPad suddenly shows a green light.

00:39:22.625 --> 00:39:25.936
It's a message from the weasel.

00:39:25.996 --> 00:39:30.116
Hosek going, he writes.

00:39:30.176 --> 00:39:33.067
Nothing happening yet.

00:39:33.127 --> 00:39:35.122
I write back.

00:39:35.182 --> 00:39:39.540
Are the garden flowers all right?

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:43.234
Yes, they're beautiful.

00:39:43.294 --> 00:39:46.336
Thank you very much.

00:39:46.396 --> 00:39:49.479
No problem.

00:39:49.539 --> 00:39:53.672
Are you doing the night watch then?

00:39:53.732 --> 00:39:56.896
Yes, boring.

00:39:56.956 --> 00:39:59.150
What are you doing?

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:06.575
Talking to a guy in California about the new Internet laws in America.

00:40:06.635 --> 00:40:09.276
Bad news, eh?

00:40:09.337 --> 00:40:11.197
Very bad.

00:40:11.257 --> 00:40:13.958
We don't want these laws.

00:40:14.018 --> 00:40:18.670
The Internet must be free for everyone.

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:22.222
I look at my laptop screen.

00:40:22.282 --> 00:40:23.382
Hey!

00:40:23.442 --> 00:40:25.184
Something's happening!

00:40:25.244 --> 00:40:26.404
Must go!

00:40:26.464 --> 00:40:28.789
Talk to you later!

00:40:28.849 --> 00:40:33.145
I close my iPad and turn to my laptop.

00:40:33.205 --> 00:40:35.226
The screen is flashing.

00:40:35.286 --> 00:40:40.618
Somebody is getting into the Clayton Office computer.

00:40:40.678 --> 00:40:45.170
They get to the password for the quotations folder.

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:49.322
Yes, they break through that.

00:40:49.382 --> 00:40:53.564
Now the password for the blending style.

00:40:53.624 --> 00:40:56.456
And they're through that.

00:40:56.516 --> 00:41:02.158
Now the file is downloading to a laptop.

00:41:02.219 --> 00:41:06.812
Anna's laptop.

00:41:06.872 --> 00:41:10.484
Chapter 5.

00:41:10.544 --> 00:41:11.944
Thursday.

00:41:12.004 --> 00:41:14.736
Taking photos.

00:41:14.796 --> 00:41:17.237
I phone Jeff.

00:41:17.297 --> 00:41:19.228
Hey, Jeff.

00:41:19.288 --> 00:41:21.170
Are you asleep?

00:41:21.230 --> 00:41:23.090
I'm not now.

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:26.302
He says, what's happening?

00:41:26.362 --> 00:41:28.604
I have a name for you.

00:41:28.664 --> 00:41:30.256
Are you ready?

00:41:30.316 --> 00:41:31.946
Yes.

00:41:32.006 --> 00:41:34.308
Anna Lund.

00:41:34.368 --> 00:41:38.362
He doesn't say anything for a minute then.

00:41:38.422 --> 00:41:40.062
Anna.

00:41:40.122 --> 00:41:42.333
It's Anna.

00:41:42.394 --> 00:41:44.415
Are you sure?

00:41:44.476 --> 00:41:46.947
It's Anna's laptop.

00:41:47.007 --> 00:41:55.343
Perhaps she did it or perhaps a spark and flash friend did it but it's her laptop.

00:41:55.403 --> 00:41:57.214
Right.

00:41:57.274 --> 00:42:00.637
Jeff Clayton's voice is angry.

00:42:00.697 --> 00:42:03.600
I'm calling the police in the morning.

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:09.254
They can come to the office and when Anna walks in, wait!

00:42:09.314 --> 00:42:13.248
I say, here's the bad news.

00:42:13.308 --> 00:42:22.958
Anna, or her friend, downloaded the Blandings file, but they didn't keep the file on her laptop.

00:42:23.018 --> 00:42:26.541
It went onto a memory stick.

00:42:26.601 --> 00:42:31.332
I took some screenshots of the file download.

00:42:31.392 --> 00:42:37.903
We can show the police those, but they don't prove anything.

00:42:37.963 --> 00:42:41.984
Somebody took the file from the office computer.

00:42:42.044 --> 00:42:47.480
Yes, but the files not on her laptop now.

00:42:47.540 --> 00:42:52.868
But the download happened on her laptop, says Jeff.

00:42:52.928 --> 00:42:55.980
Of course she's the thief.

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:58.140
Listen, Jeff.

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.572
What's she going to say?

00:43:00.632 --> 00:43:03.484
She's going to say, what file?

00:43:03.544 --> 00:43:05.184
What are you talking about?

00:43:05.244 --> 00:43:08.526
Where is this file on my laptop?

00:43:08.586 --> 00:43:11.747
I don't know anything about a file.

00:43:11.807 --> 00:43:14.237
She's not stupid.

00:43:14.297 --> 00:43:17.638
And she's going to be ready for any question.

00:43:17.698 --> 00:43:19.750
She did it, yes.

00:43:19.810 --> 00:43:23.402
But we can't prove it.

00:43:23.462 --> 00:43:25.222
Yes.

00:43:25.282 --> 00:43:28.144
Yes, I see.

00:43:28.204 --> 00:43:30.945
You're right, Shirley.

00:43:31.005 --> 00:43:34.406
Jeff's voice is very unhappy.

00:43:34.466 --> 00:43:38.234
So, what are we going to do?

00:43:38.294 --> 00:43:46.695
Well, the Trojan horse was Plan A and now I have Plan B, I say.

00:43:46.755 --> 00:43:50.427
Where is the Blanding's file now?

00:43:50.487 --> 00:43:52.378
We need to know that.

00:43:52.438 --> 00:43:57.321
Maybe Spark and Flash have got the file already.

00:43:57.381 --> 00:44:05.103
But maybe they haven't and maybe Anna is meeting somebody tomorrow.

00:44:05.164 --> 00:44:10.016
So I'm going to watch Anna all day.

00:44:10.076 --> 00:44:11.607
Where does she go?

00:44:11.627 --> 00:44:14.449
Who does she meet?

00:44:14.509 --> 00:44:16.320
Okay.

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:18.512
What can I do?

00:44:18.572 --> 00:44:20.614
Asks Jeff.

00:44:20.674 --> 00:44:23.846
Go into the office tomorrow.

00:44:23.906 --> 00:44:25.137
Be normal.

00:44:25.197 --> 00:44:26.578
Be nice to Anna.

00:44:26.638 --> 00:44:28.790
Nice to everyone.

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:33.250
Talk about work, the weather, the football, whatever.

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:38.260
We know about Anna, but she doesn't know that.

00:44:38.320 --> 00:44:41.391
We don't want her to worry.

00:44:41.451 --> 00:44:43.141
Okay.

00:44:43.202 --> 00:44:44.432
I get it.

00:44:44.492 --> 00:44:45.772
Jeff says.

00:44:45.832 --> 00:44:47.883
Watch and listen.

00:44:47.943 --> 00:44:51.344
Text me when Anna does anything.

00:44:51.404 --> 00:44:56.796
When she leaves the office, goes to lunch, makes a phone call.

00:44:56.857 --> 00:45:01.979
Use my Sally Hawker mobile number.

00:45:02.039 --> 00:45:11.090
I sleep for three or four hours, then get up, drink more coffee and call a taxi.

00:45:11.150 --> 00:45:17.672
I want to be outside Anna's apartment before the buses start in the morning.

00:45:17.732 --> 00:45:21.288
I wear my student disguise.

00:45:21.348 --> 00:45:29.194
I have long hair, a big hat and a wonderful long skirt with red and yellow flowers all over it.

00:45:29.254 --> 00:45:31.486
It's a great skirt.

00:45:31.546 --> 00:45:36.630
People look at the skirt and not at the face.

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:46.962
Anna leaves her apartment at eight o'clock, walks down the street to the bus stop and gets on the first bus.

00:45:47.022 --> 00:45:49.969
There are lots of people on the bus.

00:45:50.029 --> 00:45:55.811
Anna goes upstairs and I sit downstairs.

00:45:55.871 --> 00:46:03.924
She gets off the bus and walks to the office and I'm 20 metres behind her.

00:46:03.984 --> 00:46:06.215
She doesn't meet anybody.

00:46:06.275 --> 00:46:09.396
She doesn't talk to anybody.

00:46:09.456 --> 00:46:11.937
I text Jeff.

00:46:11.997 --> 00:46:15.288
Anna arriving now.

00:46:15.348 --> 00:46:17.392
Nothing happened.

00:46:17.452 --> 00:46:20.797
A text comes back from Jeff.

00:46:20.858 --> 00:46:22.630
Okay.

00:46:22.690 --> 00:46:28.112
I find a sandwich bar not far from the Clayton office.

00:46:28.172 --> 00:46:35.228
I sit by the window and from there I can see the door of the Clayton office.

00:46:35.288 --> 00:46:37.198
I'm hungry.

00:46:37.258 --> 00:46:44.973
I eat two chicken and tomato sandwiches on brown bread and drink two cups of tea.

00:46:45.034 --> 00:46:56.060
I read on my iPad Watch the clock, and when the waiter gives me a hard look, I buy another cup of tea.

00:46:56.120 --> 00:46:59.262
Seven cups of tea.

00:46:59.322 --> 00:47:01.714
It's a long morning.

00:47:01.734 --> 00:47:06.528
A text arrives on my phone.

00:47:06.588 --> 00:47:09.911
Anna, leaving for lunch now.

00:47:09.971 --> 00:47:11.532
Everything normal.

00:47:11.592 --> 00:47:15.454
We talked about last night's football.

00:47:15.514 --> 00:47:22.697
When Anna comes out, I'm standing in the street looking into a shop window.

00:47:22.758 --> 00:47:29.902
She walks quickly for ten minutes and then goes into a big pub by the river.

00:47:29.962 --> 00:47:35.647
It's lunchtime and the pub is noisy, full of people.

00:47:35.708 --> 00:47:41.090
I go in at another door and look around for Anna.

00:47:41.150 --> 00:47:46.062
She's with a man, a tall man with red hair.

00:47:46.122 --> 00:47:49.183
Is he just a friend or?

00:47:49.244 --> 00:47:52.886
No, he's more than a friend.

00:47:52.946 --> 00:47:55.078
Anna is kissing him.

00:47:55.138 --> 00:47:57.310
He's kissing her back.

00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:00.112
He has his arm around her.

00:48:00.172 --> 00:48:04.755
They're excited, happy, laughing.

00:48:04.816 --> 00:48:08.398
I move a little nearer to them.

00:48:08.458 --> 00:48:15.848
I get my phone out, hold the phone to my ear, but take photos, lots of them.

00:48:15.908 --> 00:48:17.890
Nobody looks at me.

00:48:17.950 --> 00:48:25.068
I'm just a student in a long flowery skirt, talking on my mobile phone.

00:48:25.128 --> 00:48:31.231
I take a taxi back to my office and sit down at my big laptop.

00:48:31.291 --> 00:48:34.442
I download the photos to the laptop.

00:48:34.502 --> 00:48:37.624
The camera in my phone is a good one.

00:48:37.685 --> 00:48:40.986
And these photos are really good.

00:48:41.046 --> 00:48:45.728
So, who is this guy with Anna?

00:48:45.788 --> 00:48:53.906
15 people work for Spark and Flash Design and I have all the names.

00:48:53.966 --> 00:48:57.589
I begin with the people at the top of the company.

00:48:57.649 --> 00:49:02.300
Put their names into Google and hit search.

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:05.822
I'm looking for photographs.

00:49:05.882 --> 00:49:07.762
Yes!

00:49:07.822 --> 00:49:09.333
Got him.

00:49:09.393 --> 00:49:17.306
The third name brings up lots of photographs of a tall man with red hair.

00:49:17.366 --> 00:49:22.258
He has a Facebook page with all his photos on it.

00:49:22.318 --> 00:49:24.625
Stupid man.

00:49:24.685 --> 00:49:31.824
Steven Nyberg, Director, Spark and Flash Design.

00:49:31.884 --> 00:49:36.594
I meet Jeff Clayton in Richmond Park again.

00:49:36.654 --> 00:49:43.076
This time, it's raining, so we sit in his car and make a plan.

00:49:43.136 --> 00:49:46.906
Plan C.

00:49:46.966 --> 00:49:53.999
Chapter 6, Friday, the end of the road.

00:49:54.059 --> 00:50:02.365
Early Friday morning, I'm in the sandwich bar near the Clayton office waiting for a text from Jeff.

00:50:02.425 --> 00:50:07.308
I'm early, so I have a chicken and tomato sandwich again.

00:50:07.368 --> 00:50:09.570
They're good sandwiches.

00:50:09.630 --> 00:50:12.252
A text arrives from Jeff.

00:50:12.312 --> 00:50:13.773
She's here.

00:50:13.833 --> 00:50:15.594
Come in now.

00:50:15.654 --> 00:50:20.688
Today, I'm Shirley Holmes, private investigator.

00:50:20.748 --> 00:50:23.770
quiet, dark clothes.

00:50:23.830 --> 00:50:28.874
No flowery skirts, no journalist smiley face.

00:50:28.934 --> 00:50:33.990
I walk through the Clayton office to Jeff's little room at the back.

00:50:34.050 --> 00:50:37.182
Ken and Harry stare at me.

00:50:37.243 --> 00:50:41.046
Do they see Sally Hawker or Shirley Holmes?

00:50:41.106 --> 00:50:42.678
They're not sure.

00:50:42.738 --> 00:50:48.168
In Jeff's office, I sit down on a chair near his desk.

00:50:48.228 --> 00:50:51.536
Anna is sitting in front of the desk.

00:50:51.596 --> 00:50:57.752
She looks at me quickly, then back at Jeff behind the desk.

00:50:57.812 --> 00:51:01.285
What's all this about, Jeff?

00:51:01.345 --> 00:51:02.526
She says.

00:51:02.586 --> 00:51:05.098
And why is Sally Hawker here?

00:51:05.118 --> 00:51:09.382
Anna, Jeff says slowly.

00:51:09.442 --> 00:51:13.755
He has Anna's laptop on his desk in front of him.

00:51:13.816 --> 00:51:17.014
He puts his hands on it.

00:51:17.074 --> 00:51:25.656
On Wednesday night this week, you downloaded a file from the office computer in the middle of the night.

00:51:25.716 --> 00:51:35.063
You used your laptop, that is my laptop, the Clayton Electric's laptop, in your home.

00:51:35.124 --> 00:51:43.564
You broke through the computer security and that's two passwords to take this file.

00:51:43.624 --> 00:51:46.482
What did you do with that file?

00:51:46.542 --> 00:51:48.984
Where is it now?

00:51:49.044 --> 00:51:50.774
Anna loves.

00:51:50.835 --> 00:51:53.866
Hey, Jeff.

00:51:53.926 --> 00:51:56.248
What is all this?

00:51:56.308 --> 00:51:58.520
I'm your best designer.

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:00.132
You tell me that.

00:52:00.192 --> 00:52:03.143
What file are you talking about?

00:52:03.163 --> 00:52:07.316
I didn't know anything about a file.

00:52:07.377 --> 00:52:11.776
Jeff looks at a piece of paper on his desk.

00:52:11.836 --> 00:52:21.275
I have here a screenshot of your laptop at 1.05am 24th of May.

00:52:21.336 --> 00:52:30.359
It shows the download of a file, a very important file with information about Costings.

00:52:30.419 --> 00:52:33.280
What did you do with it?

00:52:33.340 --> 00:52:37.072
Who did you sell this file to?

00:52:37.132 --> 00:52:40.003
Anna isn't laughing now.

00:52:40.063 --> 00:52:42.368
She looks quickly at me.

00:52:42.428 --> 00:52:46.434
Then away again, she's worried.

00:52:46.495 --> 00:52:48.626
Why am I here?

00:52:48.686 --> 00:52:51.838
Why am I not speaking?

00:52:51.898 --> 00:52:54.148
She doesn't like it.

00:52:54.208 --> 00:52:59.180
I say nothing, just sit and watch her.

00:52:59.240 --> 00:53:01.772
She looks at Jeff.

00:53:01.832 --> 00:53:06.207
Look, Jeff, she says.

00:53:06.267 --> 00:53:09.110
I don't like this.

00:53:09.170 --> 00:53:11.421
What are you saying?

00:53:11.481 --> 00:53:14.322
Are you calling me a thief?

00:53:14.382 --> 00:53:16.572
Yes, I am.

00:53:16.632 --> 00:53:19.143
Who did you sell the file to?

00:53:19.203 --> 00:53:20.313
Jeff says.

00:53:20.373 --> 00:53:25.004
Was it to someone from Spark and Flash design maybe?

00:53:25.024 --> 00:53:27.905
What?

00:53:27.965 --> 00:53:32.816
I don't know anybody from Spark and Flash design.

00:53:32.876 --> 00:53:36.470
Who or what are they?

00:53:36.530 --> 00:53:42.688
Oh dear, oh dear, bad mistake, Anna.

00:53:42.748 --> 00:53:48.942
I take out my photographs and push them across the desk to her.

00:53:49.002 --> 00:53:52.426
Big colour photos.

00:53:52.486 --> 00:53:59.370
Stephen Nyberg, director, spark and flash design, I say.

00:53:59.431 --> 00:54:02.878
Steve, to his friends.

00:54:02.938 --> 00:54:08.674
Anna looks at the photos of her and Steven Nyberg.

00:54:08.734 --> 00:54:12.415
Her face goes white, then red.

00:54:12.475 --> 00:54:14.796
She stares at me.

00:54:14.856 --> 00:54:19.248
She wants to kill me, right now.

00:54:19.308 --> 00:54:22.329
I can see it in her eyes.

00:54:22.389 --> 00:54:27.854
You are not a journalist, she says angrily.

00:54:27.914 --> 00:54:29.547
What are you?

00:54:29.607 --> 00:54:30.578
Police?

00:54:30.638 --> 00:54:34.339
Or just a dirty little investigator?

00:54:34.399 --> 00:54:42.382
I hope Nyberg paid you well for all the Clayton quotations and costings, I say.

00:54:42.442 --> 00:54:45.202
Or did you do it for love?

00:54:45.263 --> 00:54:48.294
Love is cheaper for the company.

00:54:48.354 --> 00:54:51.494
Anna gets really angry now.

00:54:51.554 --> 00:54:53.439
She turns to Jeff.

00:54:53.499 --> 00:54:56.755
You think you're very clever, don't you?

00:54:56.815 --> 00:55:00.228
You and your stupid little company.

00:55:00.288 --> 00:55:03.950
You're not going to get this Blanding's contract.

00:55:04.010 --> 00:55:07.344
I see BC don't like spending their money.

00:55:07.404 --> 00:55:10.306
They want better prices than yours.

00:55:10.366 --> 00:55:14.700
And Steve can give them those better prices.

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:18.162
Second bad mistake, Anna.

00:55:18.222 --> 00:55:20.003
I look quickly at Jeff.

00:55:20.063 --> 00:55:21.534
Did he hear it?

00:55:21.594 --> 00:55:24.076
Yes, he did.

00:55:24.136 --> 00:55:28.380
How do you know the client is ICBC?"

00:55:28.440 --> 00:55:30.912
he says.

00:55:30.972 --> 00:55:37.209
You told us Anna's face is white again.

00:55:37.229 --> 00:55:40.062
No, I didn't.

00:55:40.122 --> 00:55:41.352
Jeff says.

00:55:41.413 --> 00:55:44.105
I said it was a Chinese bank.

00:55:44.165 --> 00:55:47.118
I didn't tell you the name of the bank.

00:55:47.178 --> 00:55:55.906
I didn't tell anybody the name of the bank, but the name ICBC was in the Blandings quotation.

00:55:55.966 --> 00:55:59.188
So you read the file, didn't you?

00:55:59.248 --> 00:56:07.525
You broke the passwords, downloaded the file, read it, and gave it to Spark and Flash.

00:56:07.586 --> 00:56:09.958
You're a hacker and a thief.

00:56:10.018 --> 00:56:13.785
Do you have anything to say?

00:56:13.845 --> 00:56:16.931
Anna says nothing.

00:56:16.991 --> 00:56:19.612
There is nothing to say.

00:56:19.672 --> 00:56:24.824
She's at the end of the road and she has nowhere to go.

00:56:24.884 --> 00:56:27.754
Jeff stands up.

00:56:27.814 --> 00:56:30.656
Get out, he says.

00:56:30.716 --> 00:56:33.206
Take your things and get out.

00:56:33.266 --> 00:56:37.068
Sally, please go with her and watch her.

00:56:37.128 --> 00:56:42.658
I want her out of this building in five minutes.

00:56:42.718 --> 00:56:50.411
Chapter 7 Friday, an evening out.

00:56:50.472 --> 00:57:04.080
In the afternoon, I'm back in my office writing emails, checking messages, doing the paperwork, the phone rings, my Sally Hawker mobile.

00:57:04.140 --> 00:57:07.812
Hey, Shirley, Jeff here.

00:57:07.872 --> 00:57:10.944
Can I come and see you?

00:57:11.004 --> 00:57:12.692
Sure.

00:57:12.752 --> 00:57:16.069
He arrives ten minutes later.

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:18.150
Big, happy smile.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:21.431
His eyes don't look worried now.

00:57:21.491 --> 00:57:25.872
Got some news, he says happily.

00:57:25.932 --> 00:57:29.332
I wanted you to be the first to know.

00:57:29.352 --> 00:57:33.143
Well, that's nice, I say.

00:57:33.203 --> 00:57:35.704
Is it exciting news?

00:57:35.764 --> 00:57:37.492
He laughs.

00:57:37.552 --> 00:57:41.272
It is for Clayton Electric's.

00:57:41.332 --> 00:57:44.603
We've got the Blanding's house contract.

00:57:44.663 --> 00:57:46.234
I heard this afternoon.

00:57:46.254 --> 00:57:48.385
Wow!

00:57:48.445 --> 00:57:50.396
That's great news!

00:57:50.456 --> 00:57:51.796
Well done!

00:57:51.857 --> 00:57:53.067
I say.

00:57:53.127 --> 00:57:57.629
So Spark and Flash didn't do a quotation.

00:57:57.689 --> 00:57:59.980
Or they just didn't get it?

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:01.731
What happened?

00:58:01.791 --> 00:58:03.740
I don't know.

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:08.176
says Jeff, but I did two quotations.

00:58:08.236 --> 00:58:12.066
I put one on the office computer on Wednesday.

00:58:12.127 --> 00:58:20.255
You know, when you told me to, but I did a second quotation with much better prices.

00:58:20.316 --> 00:58:26.880
I didn't put that on any office computer or send it by email.

00:58:26.940 --> 00:58:32.414
I put it on paper and sent it by motorbike messenger.

00:58:32.475 --> 00:58:37.308
Nobody in the office ever saw that quotation.

00:58:37.368 --> 00:58:39.589
I laughed.

00:58:39.649 --> 00:58:42.641
Very clever!

00:58:42.661 --> 00:58:45.253
I didn't think of that.

00:58:45.313 --> 00:58:49.736
And did ICBC like those prices then?

00:58:49.797 --> 00:58:51.888
He looks at me.

00:58:51.948 --> 00:58:57.994
You opened the file in the night and read the quotation, didn't you?

00:58:58.054 --> 00:59:01.356
You knew it was ICBC.

00:59:01.417 --> 00:59:02.688
Of course!

00:59:02.748 --> 00:59:05.350
I read it, I say.

00:59:05.411 --> 00:59:08.467
I needed to know.

00:59:08.527 --> 00:59:18.686
Jeff says, well Shirley Holmes, Sally Hawker, would you like to have dinner with me tonight?

00:59:18.746 --> 00:59:22.437
A big, thank you, dinner.

00:59:22.497 --> 00:59:29.339
Bread and olives at the Athena restaurant, I say, he smiles.

00:59:29.399 --> 00:59:33.140
He has a really nice smile.

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:37.984
There's a little French restaurant near Richmond Bridge.

00:59:38.044 --> 00:59:40.038
Right by the river.

00:59:40.098 --> 00:59:42.560
Very good food.

00:59:42.620 --> 00:59:47.254
They don't do bread and olives.

00:59:47.314 --> 00:59:51.367
We're meeting at seven o'clock.

00:59:51.427 --> 00:59:56.752
At home, I'm getting ready when I suddenly remember.

00:59:56.812 --> 00:59:58.683
It's Friday night.

00:59:58.743 --> 01:00:01.412
Sahid's chicken dinner.

01:00:01.472 --> 01:00:06.273
I phone Layla, but Zahid answers the phone.

01:00:06.333 --> 01:00:10.626
He's home early because he's doing the cooking.

01:00:10.686 --> 01:00:14.018
This is going to be difficult.

01:00:14.078 --> 01:00:16.350
Zahid!

01:00:16.410 --> 01:00:22.754
I say, I'm really sorry, but I can't come tonight.

01:00:22.814 --> 01:00:25.391
I'm meeting a client.

01:00:25.451 --> 01:00:30.159
On Friday evening, Saheed says.

01:00:30.220 --> 01:00:32.651
That was last Friday.

01:00:32.711 --> 01:00:34.052
Leila told me.

01:00:34.072 --> 01:00:37.714
The client wants me to have dinner with him.

01:00:37.734 --> 01:00:39.375
To say thank you.

01:00:39.395 --> 01:00:41.827
I said.

01:00:41.887 --> 01:00:45.679
You never do that usually, Shirley.

01:00:45.740 --> 01:00:48.501
You never have dinner with clients.

01:00:48.561 --> 01:00:51.093
Clients are work, you say.

01:00:51.153 --> 01:00:54.748
Not friends to have dinner with.

01:00:54.808 --> 01:01:01.644
But this client has very beautiful brown eyes, Saheed.

01:01:01.705 --> 01:01:06.678
I just want to look into his eyes one more time.

01:01:06.698 --> 01:01:11.200
I enjoy teasing Saheed.

01:01:11.260 --> 01:01:14.702
Suddenly he understands.

01:01:14.763 --> 01:01:17.724
Ah, okay.

01:01:17.784 --> 01:01:21.646
This client has nice brown eyes.

01:01:21.706 --> 01:01:25.837
But can he cook a chicken dinner?

01:01:25.897 --> 01:01:29.918
Of course not, I say.

01:01:29.978 --> 01:01:35.170
Your chicken dinners are the best in London, Said.

01:01:35.230 --> 01:01:37.160
And I want some.

01:01:37.220 --> 01:01:39.912
Can you keep some for me?

01:01:39.972 --> 01:01:42.693
Can I come around on Saturday?

01:01:42.713 --> 01:01:46.695
Eight o'clock, he says.

01:01:46.755 --> 01:01:49.336
And don't be late.

